# =============================================================================
# Rise Next Banking CRM — FRONTEND environment
# =============================================================================
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local      (run from the frontend/ directory)
#
# ⚠️  EVERY VARIABLE HERE IS PUBLIC. `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` is substituted into the
#     JavaScript bundle at build time and is readable by anyone who loads the
#     page. **Nothing secret may ever be put in this file or in the Vercel
#     environment as a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable.** Secrets belong to the backend
#     (backend/.env.example), which the browser never sees.
#
# Because they are baked in at BUILD time, changing any of these in Vercel
# requires a **redeploy** — restarting is not enough and there is nothing at
# runtime to reload.
#
# `frontend/.gitignore` covers `.env*.local`; the root `.gitignore` covers a
# plain `.env` at any depth. `.env.example` is explicitly allowed through.
# =============================================================================


# ------------------------------------------------------------------- api ----

# REQUIRED in production. Base URL of the Railway backend, no trailing slash.
# Read by src/lib/api.ts, which appends `/api` itself — so give the ORIGIN
# only, not `.../api`.
#
#   Local:      http://localhost:8080
#   Production: https://<your-service>.up.railway.app
#
# ⚠️  Two things must agree with this value or authentication breaks in a way
#     that looks like a bug rather than a misconfiguration:
#       · the backend's CORS_ORIGIN must name THIS app's origin, and
#       · the backend's NODE_ENV must be `production`, or the refresh cookie is
#         issued `SameSite=Lax` and the browser will never send it across the
#         Vercel↔Railway boundary. Sign-in appears to work and every reload
#         signs the user out.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL="http://localhost:8080"


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ demo ----

# CLIENT-PRESENTATION DEMO — build-time switch.
#
# Decides whether the demo module (frontend/src/lib/demo/) is compiled into the
# bundle at all. Read only by next.config.ts, at build time; no client code
# reads it. Not a secret.
#
#   unset   →  follows NODE_ENV:  `next dev` includes the demo,
#                                 `next build` EXCLUDES it.
#   "true"  →  included, even in a production build. Use this to produce a
#              deployable build for a client walkthrough.
#   other   →  excluded.
#
# ⚠️  LEAVE THIS UNSET FOR A REAL DEPLOYMENT. When excluded, `@/lib/demo` is
#     aliased to `@/lib/demo-disabled`, so the demo credential and every
#     fabricated customer record are absent from the shipped JavaScript — not
#     merely unreachable within it. SEC-001/SEC-027 are about exactly this, and
#     `npm run verify:demo-exclusion` is what proves it on both bundlers.
#
# See docs/DECISIONS.md D-014 and the "Demo mode" section of README.md.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ENABLE_DEMO="true"


# --------------------------------------------------------- observability ----

# Browser error reporting — Task 15.5. OPTIONAL.
#
# Unset, nothing is transmitted: the error boundaries still render an honest
# screen with the Next.js digest for the user to quote, and no report leaves the
# browser.
#
# Set it and a crash POSTs one fixed JSON envelope there via `sendBeacon`.
#
# ⚠️  THIS URL IS PUBLIC, so there is deliberately NO TOKEN to go with it. A
#     credential compiled into a bundle is not a credential. The endpoint must
#     therefore accept unauthenticated posts and be rate-limited on its own
#     side — treat it as a write-only sink and nothing more.
#
# ⚠️  WHAT IS SENT: the error name, message, stack and digest; the PATHNAME; the
#     environment and release. **Never the query string** — `/accept-invite` and
#     `/reset-password` carry a live single-use token there, and a crash on
#     either page would otherwise post it to a third party. No user id, no
#     email, no session data.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ERROR_TRACKING_URL="https://collector.example.com/browser"

# Optional. The commit SHA of the running build, reported in the error envelope
# and nowhere else. Not a secret. On Vercel, set it to $VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_RELEASE_SHA=
